    "notes": [
      "Religion shares checked against Pew Research Center's state table of Census 2011: agree within rounding.",
      "Corrected (major): 'loramhoushü' was filed as a men's shawl, but Wikipedia's Loramhoushü and Naga shawl pages describe it as a women's wrap-around skirt, white with broad black, green and red bands. Replaced by a women's festive item (nl_angami_loram_f); no character used the old item.",
      "Corrected: the Angami lohe (lohepfhe) is black with red and green bands (yellow only in older pieces), per Wikipedia; the prompt said red and yellow.",
      "Confirmed: the Ao tsungkotepsü was traditionally earned (warriors, later feasts of merit), so keeping it restricted is right (Wikipedia's Naga shawl page). Chakhesang shawl is a Nagaland GI product.",
      "Confirmed against Wikipedia's List of traditional Naga festivals: Sekrenyi in February, Moatsü first week of May, Tülüni in July, Monyü first week of April; Hornbill Festival, Tokhü Emong, Aoleang and Sükhrünyie dates made precise (Hornbill 1-10 December). Bushu (Dimasa Kachari) is not in that list and stays single-source."
    ]
  }
